lesson:
C++ is the language under most big games (Unreal Engine is written in it and scripted in it), plus robotics, Arduino and anything where speed matters. It's stricter and more manual than Python, which is exactly why it teaches you how computers really work.

```
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
    std::cout << "Hello, world!\n";
    std::cout << "2 + 3 = " << 2 + 3 << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

- `#include <iostream>` brings in the input/output library.
- The program starts in `main`. `return 0;` tells the system "finished fine".
- `std::cout` is the screen. `<<` sends things to it, one after another.
- `"\n"` starts a new line. (`std::endl` does too, but it's slower.)
- `std::` means "from the standard library". Some tutorials write `using namespace std;` to skip it; real projects (and Unreal) avoid that, so this course writes `std::`.

C++ is **compiled**: a compiler (g++) turns your file into a program, then you run that program. If it doesn't compile, read the **first** error first; later errors are often caused by it.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
    std::cout << "Hello, world!\n";
    std::cout << "2 + 3 = " << 2 + 3 << "\n";
    std::cout << "4" << "2" << "\n";
    return 0;
}

